New Year policy speech: explicit growth/distribution program, wage increases, minimum wage, and SME pass-through.

REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0056"],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}
June 2022 action plan: concrete multi-year plans for startups, green transformation, and asset-income reforms.

REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0061"],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}
October 2022 package: large-scale fiscal response to prices, with explicit GDP and household-support aims.

REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0080"],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.4","8B.6","8B.8"]}
Reskilling and permanent-employment investment: concrete people-investment implementation.

REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0026"],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.5","8B.9","8B.10"]}
December 2022 security documents: cabinet-approved strategy, defense buildup, and stable financing plan.

**New Evidence**
PMO reshuffle and emergency cabinet:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-08-10","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202208/_00007.html","claim":"Kishida said he launched a reshuffled cabinet to move decisively on emergencies, retained the basic framework of his administration, and appointed an experienced, highly capable ministerial lineup while making defense reinforcement a priority.","locator":"opening statement, lines 23-33","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct prime-ministerial record of a real cabinet reshuffle and stated priorities; the personnel move itself is observable and attributable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Strong for Kishida's appointment choices and sequencing of priorities; actual downstream performance still depends on ministers and agencies.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-described capability, not independent proof of execution quality.","Later controversy around some ministers' external ties does not negate the reshuffle but limits a pure competence reading."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.4","8B.5","8B.9","8B.10"]}

Startup plan and implementation machinery:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sub-committee for Fostering Startups","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-11-24","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/actions/202211/_00032.html","claim":"Kishida attended the third meeting of the startup subcommittee, where participants discussed a draft Startup Development Five-year Plan.","locator":"lines 40-42","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official meeting record documenting the ruler's attendance and the concrete plan under discussion.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Strong for agenda-setting and cabinet/working-level coordination; weak for later outcomes because the page records plan discussion, not delivery.","contrary_evidence":["The page shows planning, not implementation or outcome.","Startup effects would depend on later follow-through by ministries and the private sector."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.5","8B.6","8B.8","8B.10"]}

Passing bills, support architecture, and weekly monitoring:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Press Conference by Prime Minister Kishida","publisher":"Prime Minister's Office of Japan","publication_date":"2022-12-10","url":"https://japan.kantei.go.jp/101_kishida/statement/202212/_00004.html","claim":"Kishida said the extraordinary Diet session passed almost all government bills, including the second supplementary budget for fiscal 2022 and the amended Infectious Diseases Control Law, and he created a government-wide team plus weekly progress checks to get the new system operating reliably and the economic package implemented quickly.","locator":"opening statement, lines 35-41 and 55-63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct record of enacted legislation, coordination mechanisms, and monitoring instructions from the prime minister.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Strong for Kishida's coordination and enforcement choices; actual service delivery and macro impact still depend on implementation by ministries and local governments.","contrary_evidence":["The law and team show implementation architecture, not proof of field-level effectiveness.","Some benefits, such as wage growth and price relief, were still pending or incomplete by year-end."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.5","8B.6","8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}

Minimum wage move:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan's planned record minimum wage hike opens path to sustained GDP growth","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-08-01","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/japans-average-minimum-wage-set-to-rise-at-record-pace-this-year-nikkei-2859912","claim":"Reuters reported that Japan's average minimum wage was set to rise by a record 31 yen, or 3.3 percent, to 961 yen per hour, and framed the move as supportive of Kishida's effort to cushion households from commodity inflation.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'TOKYO (Reuters)...'","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent contemporaneous reporting on an official wage recommendation with a clear policy link to Kishida's stated agenda.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to strong: Kishida championed higher wages, but the figure itself came through the ministry committee process, not unilateral PM decree.","contrary_evidence":["Higher minimum wages do not guarantee broad real-wage gains.","The same year inflation still eroded purchasing power."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

Inflation package:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan unveils $200 billion in new spending to ease inflation pain","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/japan-to-compile-200-billion-extra-budget-for-spending-package-2924935","claim":"Reuters reported that Japan would spend about $200 billion on an economic stimulus package to tame inflation and cushion the blow from higher raw material costs.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'TOKYO (Reuters)...'","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent contemporaneous reporting on the cabinet-approved package and its stated purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Strong for Kishida because the package was the cabinet's response under his premiership, but the shock environment was externally driven.","contrary_evidence":["The package responds to imported inflation and a weak yen rather than evidence of autonomous economic strength.","Its size also raises questions about targeting and fiscal sustainability."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

Real wages:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan FY2022 real wages fall most in 8 years, outlook seen brighter","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2023-05-22","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/japan-fiscal-2022-real-wages-fall-most-in-8-years-as-inflation-bites-3088242","claim":"Reuters reported that Japan's inflation-adjusted real wages fell the most in eight years in fiscal 2022 because inflation outpaced nominal wage gains.","locator":"paragraphs 420-431","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent government-data-based reporting on a hard outcome relevant to Kishida's wage-and-distribution agenda.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on the 2022 period","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the wage/inflation mix reflects Kishida's policy choices but also energy prices, exchange rates, and Bank of Japan policy.","contrary_evidence":["Nominal wages did rise, so the failure was partial rather than absolute.","The article notes outlook improvement later, showing the 2022 result was not necessarily durable."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}

IMF agenda framing:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan: Staff Concluding Statement of the 2022 Article IV Mission","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-01-27","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/01/27/mcs012722-japan-staff-concluding-statement-of-the-2022-article-iv-mission","claim":"IMF staff said Japan's government economic agenda was centered on a cycle of growth and distribution, with priority policies including digital transformation, green investment, startup support, regional digitalization, and economic security, while income distribution policy targeted wage hikes and human-capital investment.","locator":"lines 294-307","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment summarizing the government's agenda and implementation instruments using contemporaneous policy analysis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"early 2022 policy baseline and program framing","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to strong for Kishida's agenda-setting; weaker for execution because the statement evaluates policy design more than outcomes.","contrary_evidence":["IMF also said some support could have been better targeted.","This is mostly agenda and design evidence, not proof of delivery."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.7"]}

OECD 2023 outlook:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2023 Issue 1","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2023-06-07","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2023/issue-1_ce188438-en/full-report/component-33.html","claim":"OECD said the October 2022 economic package, plus later measures, would support households coping with the energy price shock and boost consumption and investment, while also warning that energy subsidies should become more targeted.","locator":"Japan section, lines 4038-4039 and 4081-4086","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent policy outlook using macro and program data; it directly evaluates the likely effects of 2022 measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on 2022 measures with 2023-24 trajectory","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the package is Kishida-era policy, but effects are also shaped by inflation, energy prices, and external demand.","contrary_evidence":["OECD explicitly says the subsidies should be more targeted.","The report projects continued high debt and only partial wage pass-through."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

Children and Families Agency:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Outline of the Act Establishing the Children and Families Agency (Act No. 75 of 2022)","publisher":"Children and Families Agency","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.cfa.go.jp/en/act-establishing-the-CFA-en","claim":"The act established the Children and Families Agency as a Cabinet Office organ tasked with child welfare, child health, parenting support, child advocacy, and related policy coordination.","locator":"Purpose and outline section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official legal/administrative page summarizing the enacted institutional change and its mandate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"passed in 2022; effective April 1, 2023","ruler_attribution":"Strong for Kishida because the institutional reform was part of his child-policy agenda, though actual service delivery depended on later implementation.","contrary_evidence":["The page documents the institution, not its performance.","Implementation effects are mostly visible after the 2022 period."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.9","8B.10"]}

Child welfare reform:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"令和4年6月に成立した改正児童福祉法について","publisher":"Children and Families Agency","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.cfa.go.jp/policies/jidougyakutai/Revised-Child-Welfare-Act","claim":"The June 2022 revised Child Welfare Act strengthened comprehensive local support for at-risk children and families by adding municipal support functions, child-family centers, and a certified qualification for child-welfare professionals.","locator":"overview text and outline section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official agency summary of a passed law and its implementation architecture.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Strong for Kishida because the law reflects his child-and-family agenda and a concrete legislative result, though local uptake still varies.","contrary_evidence":["The page itself notes the broader environment remained difficult and that support effects had been limited.","The law is an institutional fix, not proof of immediate welfare gains."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I screened roughly 30 documents across official Japanese sources, IMF/OECD, Reuters syndications, and secondary reporting. I kept the most non-duplicative 2022 evidence and rejected or de-prioritized the rest for one of four reasons: they repeated the same measure without adding new implementation detail, they were too generic/country-level to attribute to Kishida, they were later out-of-period material used only as optional retrospective background, or they were weaker duplications of a better official/independent source already retained.

The main documents considered included PMO speeches and action pages from January, June, August, October, November, and December 2022; Children and Families Agency law pages; the Digital Agency; MHLW wage data; IMF Article IV materials; OECD 2023 and 2024 outlook/survey pages; Reuters reports on minimum wage, stimulus, and real wages; and corroborating Japan Times, Nippon.com, Kyodo, AP, and other syndications.

I rejected or left unused several items because they were duplicates or weaker mirrors of retained evidence: multiple Reuters syndications on the same wage and stimulus stories; the MHLW minimum-wage notice as a duplicate of the Reuters wage story; later OECD 2024-2026 pages as out-of-period for direct 2022 evidence; and generic baseline indicators already captured in the local structured prior, which I did not re-fetch.

**Remaining Gaps**
- I still lack independent 2022 evidence on how much of the startup, digital, and green-transformation agenda was actually delivered by year-end rather than simply planned.
- I still lack clean causal attribution separating Kishida’s policy effects from BOJ policy, exchange rates, imported energy prices, and post-pandemic normalization on wages and inflation.
- I have good official evidence on coordination and follow-through, but limited external audit-style evidence on whether the weekly monitoring and government-wide team materially improved execution.
- More search would help, especially in Cabinet Office, METI, MIC, and budget-execution records, plus independent 2023 follow-through on startup formation, digital adoption, and regional investment.

No score assigned.
